Subject: [PATCH] ksmbd: preserve transform headers in SMB Direct writes

SMB Direct must omit the four-byte RFC1002 prefix, not the entire first
response vector. Unencrypted responses put only that prefix in iov[0],
but smb3_encrypt_resp() replaces it with the prefix followed by the
52-byte SMB3 transform header. Requiring a four-byte first vector rejects
these encrypted responses with -EINVAL before sending them.

Start the vector cursor four bytes into the first vector, advancing to
the next vector only when the prefix occupied the whole first vector.
This preserves the transform header and the existing plaintext layout
without copying or changing the caller's vectors. Reject truncated
framing and invalid vector counts before subtracting the prefix length.

--- a/fs/smb/server/transport_rdma.c
+++ b/fs/smb/server/transport_rdma.c
@@ -1518,11 +1518,18 @@ static int smb_direct_writev(struct ksmbd_transport *t,
 	if (sc->status != SMBDIRECT_SOCKET_CONNECTED)
 		return -ENOTCONN;
 
-	//FIXME: skip RFC1002 header..
-	if (WARN_ON_ONCE(niovs <= 1 || iov[0].iov_len != 4))
+	/*
+	 * Skip only the RFC1002 prefix, not payload in the first vector.
+	 * Encrypted responses also put their SMB3 transform header here.
+	 */
+	if (WARN_ON_ONCE(niovs < 1 || buflen < 4 || iov[0].iov_len < 4))
 		return -EINVAL;
 	buflen -= 4;
-	iov_idx = 1;
-	iov_ofs = 0;
+	iov_idx = 0;
+	iov_ofs = 4;
+	if (iov[0].iov_len == 4) {
+		iov_idx = 1;
+		iov_ofs = 0;
+	}
 
 	remaining_data_length = buflen;
